  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA weak cold front, currently displaced to our north and west, \nwill slowly drift south and east through the region today, \nstalling just to our south by later this afternoon. Meanwhile, a\nsurface low tracking across the boundary will eject out of the \nOhio Valley and into the northern Mid-Atlantic region. Taking a \nmore northerly track than previously depicted by the guidance, \nthis low will join with the stalled front and a couple of \nprogressive shortwaves aloft to force a more widespread area of\nshowers to develop across areas within and south of the Capital\nRegion this afternoon into tonight. The most consistent area of\nshowers with the heaviest QPF will develop in the Mid-Hudson \nValley and southwestern New England where the best forcing and \nmoisture content will be focused. The farther north, the lighter\nand more scattered showers will become as a nearby surface high\nwill provide counteracting subsidence and dry air. By late\ntonight (early Friday morning), rainfall amounts look to total\nabout 0.05\" across portions of the Capital Region to as much as\n0.8\" in far southern Dutchess and Litchfield Counties. And while\nthis will be a beneficial rain, isolated flash flooding could \nbecome a concern in these areas should the low and axis of \nheavies rain shift even farther north. \n\nAfter a dry end to the week on Friday, the chances for\nadditional rounds of showers increase once again for the\nweekend. There continues to be some uncertainty pertaining to\nthe exact rainfall amounts and locations of such due to minor \ndiscontinuities in the guidance regarding the overall set up. \nHowever, confidence is high that this weekend won't be a \ncomplete washout. What appears to be the most likely solution at\nthis point is an initial batch of showers, mainly for areas \nsouth and east of Albany Saturday afternoon through Saturday \nevening due to a surface low passing by the Long Island and New \nEngland Coastlines. Then, a second, more widespread round of\nshowers comes Sunday as a deepening cutoff low sinks into the\neastern Great Lakes. The persistence of this cutoff low \noverhead would lead to continuous moist, cyclonic flow heading \ninto the new work week which would allow for additional \nscattered showers, particularly for higher terrain areas. Once\nagain, this rain would mostly be beneficial. However, with the\npotential for showers to repeat in similar locations, there\ncould be some isolated flash flooding concerns. Confidence is\nnot high in this element of the forecast, however, so we will\nkeep an eye on trends moving forward.",
